1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,978
Every person who builds their first agent asks some version of this question within

2
00:00:04,978 --> 00:00:05,814
the first week.

3
00:00:05,814 --> 00:00:09,215
They want a date they can circle: Friday, it runs on its own.

4
00:00:09,215 --> 00:00:13,843
The honest answer is that trust is not a date, it is a ladder with three rungs, and

5
00:00:13,843 --> 00:00:18,192
you climb it by watching what actually happens, not by waiting out a calendar.

6
00:00:18,192 --> 00:00:20,590
Why does the date instinct feel so natural?

7
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:23,991
Because that is how every other tool you have adopted worked.

8
00:00:23,991 --> 00:00:28,507
You used the new spreadsheet software for a week, nothing exploded, done: trusted

9
00:00:28,507 --> 00:00:28,953
forever.

10
00:00:28,953 --> 00:00:32,689
That worked because the spreadsheet does the same thing every time.

11
00:00:32,689 --> 00:00:36,871
An agent judges, and the only way to trust judgement is the way you trust a

12
00:00:36,871 --> 00:00:38,376
person's: by keeping score.

13
00:00:38,376 --> 00:00:41,332
Two numbers, kept honestly, replace the circled date.

14
00:00:41,332 --> 00:00:43,618
How many outputs have I actually checked?

15
00:00:43,618 --> 00:00:45,792
And how many times did one surprise me?

16
00:00:45,792 --> 00:00:50,364
When the second number has been zero for long enough to mean something, you climb.

17
00:00:50,364 --> 00:00:52,929
When it stops being zero, you climb back down.

18
00:00:52,929 --> 00:00:54,546
That is the entire mechanism.

19
00:00:55,096 --> 00:00:56,974
Trial is the slow, deliberate rung.

20
00:00:56,974 --> 00:00:59,551
You are not testing whether the agent is clever.

21
00:00:59,551 --> 00:01:03,898
You are testing whether you understand its failure modes well enough to recognise

22
00:01:03,898 --> 00:01:04,972
one when it happens.

23
00:01:04,972 --> 00:01:08,729
Section 7 gives you the actual checklist for running a trial properly.

24
00:01:08,729 --> 00:01:13,130
Review is where most of the time saving shows up, because you are sampling instead

25
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:14,310
of reading everything.

26
00:01:14,310 --> 00:01:18,550
Release is the rung people picture when they imagine "having an agent." It only

27
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:22,093
works because of the two rungs underneath it, not instead of them.

28
00:01:22,093 --> 00:01:23,649
How long does each rung take?

29
00:01:23,649 --> 00:01:26,601
There is no course wide number, and that is deliberate.

30
00:01:26,601 --> 00:01:30,841
Every agent Priya onboards gets a trial sized to two things: how often the task

31
00:01:30,841 --> 00:01:33,793
runs, and how bad its worst plausible mistake would be.

32
00:01:33,793 --> 00:01:37,550
A triage agent that runs forty times a day earns its evidence quickly.

33
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:41,951
A weekly report that runs once a Monday needs several Mondays before you have seen

34
00:01:41,951 --> 00:01:42,810
anything at all.

35
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:47,318
Later sections name the trial each of her four agents actually got, and they are not

36
00:01:47,318 --> 00:01:48,177
the same number.

37
00:01:48,177 --> 00:01:52,685
If you take one rule of thumb from this lecture, take this one: count runs observed,

38
00:01:52,685 --> 00:01:53,598
not days elapsed.

39
00:01:53,598 --> 00:01:57,784
A quiet fortnight in which the agent barely ran has taught you almost nothing,

40
00:01:57,784 --> 00:01:59,233
whatever the calendar says.

41
00:01:59,783 --> 00:02:00,908
Look at the last bar.

42
00:02:00,908 --> 00:02:03,962
It does not touch zero, and that is not a drafting error.

43
00:02:03,962 --> 00:02:08,356
Section 7's spot check habit is not a training-wheels phase you eventually remove.

44
00:02:08,356 --> 00:02:09,374
It is the seatbelt.

45
00:02:09,374 --> 00:02:13,820
You wear it less often once you trust the driver, but you do not take it out of the

46
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:14,035
car.

47
00:02:14,035 --> 00:02:18,267
Why can the checking never retire completely, when it does for the spreadsheet?

48
00:02:18,267 --> 00:02:20,785
Because the world the agent reads keeps moving.

49
00:02:20,785 --> 00:02:25,179
Your clients change tone, your company renames a product, a colleague restructures

50
00:02:25,179 --> 00:02:29,679
the shared sheet, and an agent that was genuinely right for three months meets input

51
00:02:29,679 --> 00:02:30,644
it has never seen.

52
00:02:30,644 --> 00:02:31,394
Nothing broke.

53
00:02:31,394 --> 00:02:32,304
The ground moved.

54
00:02:32,304 --> 00:02:36,591
The middle bar has a practical rule inside it too: when you sample one output in

55
00:02:36,591 --> 00:02:37,608
five, sample blind.

56
00:02:37,608 --> 00:02:42,109
Pick by rolling a die, not by picking the outputs that look interesting, because the

57
00:02:42,109 --> 00:02:45,431
ones that look fine at a glance are exactly where drift hides.

58
00:02:45,981 --> 00:02:50,380
Notice the exact shape of that mistake, because it does not look like carelessness

59
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:51,239
from the inside.

60
00:02:51,239 --> 00:02:53,009
The agent's first result is good.

61
00:02:53,009 --> 00:02:57,409
Relief does the rest: relief feels like evidence, and ten right calls in the first

62
00:02:57,409 --> 00:02:58,536
week feel like proof.

63
00:02:58,536 --> 00:03:02,935
But ten right calls tell you about the ten kinds of input you happened to get that

64
00:03:02,935 --> 00:03:07,013
week, not about the kind that arrives at quarter end, or the day a colleague

65
00:03:07,013 --> 00:03:08,193
reorganises the sheet.

66
00:03:08,193 --> 00:03:12,647
The fix costs one sentence, written before the trial starts: what I must see before

67
00:03:12,647 --> 00:03:13,773
this runs without me.

68
00:03:13,773 --> 00:03:17,529
Priya's habit of saying her checks out loud is doing exactly this job.

69
00:03:17,529 --> 00:03:22,036
Decide the bar while you are calm, so a good Monday cannot quietly lower it for you.

70
00:03:22,036 --> 00:03:22,841
Hold onto that.

71
00:03:22,841 --> 00:03:27,241
Next lecture answers the question underneath the ladder: what, specifically, is an

72
00:03:27,241 --> 00:03:29,601
agent even allowed to touch while it climbs.
